# Every text file is stored and checked out with line feeds.
#
# `core.autocrlf=input` arrives at the same result, and it is a setting of the machine. It holds
# until the first checkout made without it. After that a file saved with carriage returns reaches
# a generator that moves bytes around. `scripts/sync-docs.mjs` splices the tool catalogue of a
# site page into both READMEs, and the mixture lands in a public repository as a line-ending
# change nobody asked for. `newline=""` in the Python generators stops short of this. It says how
# a file is written and says nothing about what was inside the file that was read.
#
# `text=auto` lets git tell text from binary, and `eol=lf` says how the text ones are checked
# out. The binaries below are named all the same. Git guesses well and can still be wrong: one
# control byte inside a file is enough to change its mind. Pictures, fonts and the release jar
# are here today, and the other three types are named before the first such file arrives.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff2 binary
*.jar   binary
*.zip   binary
